# 8. TECHNOLOGIES USED

## 8.1 MongoDB

MongoDB is a document-oriented database used to store application information. It supports the storage of user accounts, leads, tasks, and activity records. Its document structure allows related business information to be represented in flexible records.

## 8.2 Express.js

Express.js is a backend web framework used to define API endpoints and process HTTP requests. It connects frontend operations with business logic, authentication middleware, database models, and AI services.

## 8.3 React.js

React.js is used to develop the frontend interface. The application uses reusable components and separate pages to support authentication, dashboard visualization, lead management, pipeline monitoring, task management, and the AI assistant.

## 8.4 Node.js

Node.js provides the JavaScript runtime for the backend. It executes the server application and supports API handling, database operations, authentication, and integration with AI services.

## 8.5 Mongoose

Mongoose provides an interface between the Node.js backend and MongoDB. It defines schemas and models for application data and supports database queries and record updates.

## 8.6 JSON Web Token

JSON Web Token (JWT) supports authentication by allowing the application to issue signed tokens after successful login. The backend can validate these tokens when users access protected routes.

## 8.7 bcryptjs

bcryptjs supports password hashing. Hashing helps avoid storing user passwords directly in readable form.

## 8.8 Vite

Vite is used as the frontend development and build tool. It supports the React development workflow and produces optimized frontend assets for deployment.

## 8.9 Charting Library

The frontend uses Recharts to represent dashboard information through graphical visualizations. Charts make sales indicators and pipeline distributions easier to interpret.

## 8.10 AI Service Integration

The backend contains an AI service that supports lead scoring, sentiment analysis, email drafting, and conversational CRM assistance. The built-in engine provides rule-based and keyword-based functionality. Optional integration with external language models can provide additional capabilities when the required configuration and API credentials are available.

